<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Time series anomaly detection is critical in many real-world applications, where effective solutions must localize anomalous regions and support reliable decision-making under complex settings. However, most existing methods frame anomaly detection as a purely discriminative prediction task with fixed feature inputs, rather than an evidence-driven diagnostic process. As a result, they often struggle when anomalies exhibit strong context dependence or diverse patterns. We argue that these limitations stem from the lack of adaptive feature preparation, reasoning-aware detection, and iterative refinement during inference. To address these challenges, we propose AnomaMind, an agentic time series anomaly detection framework that reformulates anomaly detection as a sequential decision-making process. AnomaMind operates through a structured workflow that progressively localizes anomalous intervals in a coarse-to-fine manner, augments detection through multi-turn tool interactions for adaptive feature preparation, and refines anomaly decisions via self-reflection. The workflow is supported by a set of reusable tool engines, enabling context-aware diagnostic analysis. A key design of AnomaMind is an explicitly designed hybrid inference mechanism for tool-augmented anomaly detection. In this mechanism, general-purpose models are responsible for autonomous tool interaction and self-reflective refinement, while core anomaly detection decisions are learned through reinforcement learning under verifiable workflow-level feedback, enabling task-specific optimization within a flexible reasoning framework. Extensive experiments across diverse settings demonstrate that AnomaMind consistently improves anomaly detection performance. The code is available at <a href="https://github.com/Xiaoyu-Tao/AnomaMind-TS" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/Xiaoyu-Tao/AnomaMind-TS</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Anomaly Detection, Agentic Workflow, Tool-augmented Reasoning
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Time series anomaly detection (TSAD) plays a critical role in many real-world systems, where detected anomalies often trigger downstream actions such as alarms, diagnosis, or intervention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zamanzadeh Darban et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. In such settings, effective anomaly detection requires more than assigning anomaly scores to individual observations. Practical solutions need to localize anomalous intervals, interpret diagnostic evidence, and support decision-making under evolving contextual conditions with limited or uncertain information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. As time series data become increasingly diverse and context-dependent, these requirements place growing demands on anomaly detection methods beyond pure predictive accuracy, calling for evidence-driven and decision-oriented detection paradigms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schmidl et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite substantial progress <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zamanzadeh Darban et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chandola et al., 2009</a>)</cite>, most existing time series anomaly detection methods remain model-centric. They typically frame anomaly detection as a purely discriminative prediction task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al., 2013</a>)</cite> with fixed and predefined feature inputs, rather than as an evidence-driven diagnostic process. Such formulations rely on rigid feature representations and scoring mechanisms, offering limited flexibility during inference. As a result, these methods often struggle when anomalies exhibit strong context dependence, evolve over time, or require careful analysis across different temporal resolutions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. More fundamentally, they lack mechanisms to adaptively prepare informative features, reason over intermediate evidence, or revise earlier decisions as new information becomes available, which limits their effectiveness in complex real-world settings under concept shift.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We argue that these limitations stem from a fundamental mismatch between how anomaly detection is commonly formulated and how it is carried out in practice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. In real-world scenarios, anomaly detection rarely reduces to a single scoring or prediction step. Instead, it typically unfolds as a sequential decision-making process, in which analysts progressively narrow down suspicious intervals, selectively examine informative signals, and refine judgments based on intermediate findings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goswami et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Decisions made at earlier stages directly influence what evidence is gathered next and how subsequent analyses are conducted <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. However, such iterative and adaptive processes are difficult to capture within conventional model-centric formulations, which treat anomaly detection as a static mapping from inputs to anomaly scores and provide limited support for dynamic reasoning or decision revision during inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Russell-Gilbert et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. These observations motivate moving beyond isolated predictive models toward agentic time series systems that organize anomaly detection as a system-level reasoning and decision process, integrating evidence acquisition, analysis, and refinement within a coherent and adaptive workflow.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">While this perspective motivates a shift toward agentic time series systems, realizing such an anomaly detection process in practice poses substantial challenges. Effective anomaly reasoning requires access to diverse and reusable analytical capabilities, which calls for the careful design of tool engines that support adaptive feature preparation and diagnostic analysis without relying on hard-coded, task-specific pipelines <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. In addition, the detection workflow must be explicitly structured to support progressive decision-making, in which coarse localization and fine-grained analysis are coherently integrated rather than treated as isolated stages <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Such a coarse-to-fine workflow is essential for balancing efficiency, accuracy, and interpretability. Beyond workflow design, inference in agentic settings introduces fundamental challenges related to stability and optimization. While general-purpose models offer flexibility for open-ended reasoning and autonomous tool coordination, they often lack the inductive biases and task-specific learning signals required for reliable anomaly detection decisions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alnegheimish et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Ignoring these complementary strengths and limitations can lead to brittle or inefficient inference behaviors. Addressing this challenge calls for a hybrid inference paradigm that explicitly separates flexible reasoning from task-specific decision learning, combining general-purpose models with explicit and verifiable learning objectives to enable effective anomaly reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we propose AnomaMind, an agentic time series anomaly detection framework that explicitly formulates anomaly detection as a sequential decision-making process. AnomaMind operates through a structured agentic workflow that incrementally constructs diagnostic evidence and refines anomaly decisions over multiple steps. Rather than relying on fixed feature inputs, AnomaMind progressively localizes anomalous intervals in a coarse-to-fine manner and gradually augments detection via multi-turn interactions with reusable analysis tools for adaptive feature preparation and diagnostic support. Crucially, AnomaMind adopts a hybrid inference mechanism: general-purpose models handle tool invocation and self-reflective refinement, while core anomaly detection decisions are optimized through reinforcement learning under verifiable workflow-level feedback. This design enables AnomaMind to integrate flexible reasoning with task-specific optimization, while preserving effectiveness in complex anomaly detection scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our main contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We reformulate the task of TSAD as a sequential decision-making problem, moving beyond discriminative formulations toward an evidence-driven diagnostic process.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AnomaMind</span>, an agentic time series anomaly detection framework that realizes this reformulation through a structured coarse-to-fine workflow, tool-augmented adaptive feature preparation, and a hybrid inference mechanism that combines general-purpose reasoning with task-specific anomaly decision learning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct extensive experiments across diverse time series anomaly detection settings, demonstrating that AnomaMind consistently improves detection performance and validating the effectiveness of agentic, tool-augmented reasoning for TSAD.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first review conventional anomaly detection methods, and then introduce recent advances in LLM–based TSAD.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Conventional Anomaly Detection</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing time series anomaly detection methods span several modeling paradigms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al., 2013</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chandola et al., 2009</a>)</cite>. Early statistical approaches detect anomalies by assuming that normal time series follow predefined distributional or structural patterns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hyndman and Athanasopoulos, 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roberts, 2000</a>)</cite>. Classical detectors model regular temporal components such as trend and seasonality, and identify anomalies by thresholding residuals between observed values and model-based predictions, as exemplified by ARIMA-based residual analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rousseeuw and Leroy, 2003</a>)</cite>. Density-based approaches estimate data distributions or relative sample densities and treat sparse observations as anomalies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schölkopf et al., 2001</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Breunig et al., 2000</a>)</cite>.
These methods offer clear interpretability but rely heavily on handcrafted assumptions.
To alleviate this limitation, data-driven deep learning methods adopt representation learning to model temporal patterns more flexibly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Su et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. Reconstruction-based approaches, exemplified by USAD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Audibert et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, learn compact representations of normal time series using autoencoder architectures and treat large reconstruction errors as indicators of anomalies. In contrast, forecasting-based approaches, such as LSTM-based predictors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chauhan and Vig, 2015</a>)</cite>, model temporal dynamics by predicting future values from historical observations and identify anomalies through large deviations between predicted and actual outcomes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>LLM-based Anomaly Detection</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">More recently, LLM-based approaches have been explored for time series anomaly detection (TSAD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhat et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Early studies predominantly convert numerical sequences into textual representations and directly feed them into pre-trained LLMs for zero-shot or few-shot anomaly identification. Representative methods such as SigLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alnegheimish et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and prompt-based GPT-4 detectors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2026</a>)</cite> demonstrate that LLMs can capture coarse abnormal patterns without task-specific training, leveraging the rich prior knowledge. However, these detection-only approaches essentially operate as static input–output mappings, lacking explicit modeling of temporal structure and iterative diagnostic reasoning.
In contrast, training-based strategies introduce task-specific adaptation mechanisms, typically via parameter-efficient fine-tuning or learnable adapters, to better align with anomaly detection objectives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. A representative example is AnomalyLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which targets TSAD by distilling anomaly-aware representations from a pre-trained LLM into a lightweight student network and detecting anomalies through teacher–student feature discrepancies. More recently, agentic and tool-augmented LLM frameworks have been proposed to further advance TSAD by reframing detection as a multi-step decision-making process. For instance, ARGOS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> employs multiple LLM-based agents to iteratively generate, validate, and refine anomaly detection rules through interaction with external tools and contextual data sources.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.13807v1/framework_v6.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/221;" width="476" height="221" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>Overview of AnomaMind, an agentic TSAD framework that adopts a structured coarse-to-fine workflow built upon a toolkit box and supported by hybrid inference.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first formalize time series anomaly detection from a sequential decision-making perspective, and then discuss the underlying reasoning process that motivates agentic detection.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Problem Formulation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formalize time series anomaly detection as a <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sequential decision-making process</em> that unfolds over multiple interaction steps. Given an input time series <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{1:T}=\{x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{T}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{1:T}=\{x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{T}\}</annotation></semantics></math> together with optional contextual information <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{c}_{1:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}_{1:T}</annotation></semantics></math>, anomaly detection is no longer driven by fixed feature inputs. Instead, detection is carried out through a sequence of decisions under <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">evolving inputs</em>, where newly acquired evidence is incrementally incorporated during inference. Specifically, the detection process proceeds over discrete steps <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,\dots,K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,\dots,K</annotation></semantics></math>. At each step, the decision-making process operates on the information currently available, which may include the original observations, contextual signals, intermediate analysis results, and outputs produced by external analytical tools. Based on this evolving information, an action <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is selected from a structured action space. Actions may correspond to operations such as refining candidate anomalous regions, invoking diagnostic analyses, or updating anomaly judgments. Importantly, intermediate findings and tool outputs are treated as part of the effective input for subsequent steps, allowing anomaly decisions to be iteratively refined as new evidence becomes available.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Thinking Process for Anomaly Detection</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To better understand the limitations of static anomaly detection formulations, it is instructive to examine how anomaly detection is typically performed in practice. Rather than making an immediate and definitive judgment, anomaly detection often follows an iterative reasoning process that gradually constructs evidence and refines decisions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pimentel et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chandrayan et al., </a>)</cite>. The process commonly begins with coarse screening, where the goal is to quickly identify regions (or intervals) that are potentially suspicious while filtering out large portions of evidently normal behavior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. At this stage, decisions are intentionally conservative and uncertainty-aware, favoring broad candidate regions over precise localization. Subsequently, targeted analysis is performed on the selected regions. This stage involves selectively examining informative signals, such as statistical irregularities, temporal patterns, or contextual inconsistencies, to assess whether the observed behavior can be reasonably explained. Importantly, such analysis is adaptive: different regions may require different types of examination depending on their characteristics and surrounding context. Throughout this process, anomaly judgments are rarely final at the moment they are made. Early decisions are often provisional and may be revised as new evidence becomes available <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Additional analysis can either strengthen an initial suspicion or invalidate it, leading to decision refinement or rollback. This iterative reasoning process highlights that anomaly detection is fundamentally evidence-driven and decision-oriented, rather than a single-pass scoring operation. Viewed from this perspective, time series anomaly detection can be naturally formulated as a sequential procedure of candidate localization, evidence construction, and decision refinement, motivating agentic approaches that explicitly model anomaly detection as a sequential decision-making process in real-world practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>The Proposed AnomaMind</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building on the sequential decision-making formulation and evidence-driven reasoning introduced earlier, we present AnomaMind, an agentic framework for time series anomaly detection. AnomaMind operationalizes anomaly detection through adaptive feature preparation, tool-augmented reasoning, and iterative decision refinement, as detailed in the following sections.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Framework Overview</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.2. LLM-based Anomaly Detection ‣ 2. Related Work ‣ AnomaMind: Agentic Time Series Anomaly Detection with Tool-Augmented Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> illustrates the overall framework of AnomaMind. The framework is built upon a unified toolkit that provides structured analytical capabilities for perception, evidence construction, and reasoning support, including interval localization, feature extraction, and knowledge memory. Leveraging this toolkit, AnomaMind organizes anomaly detection as a progressive decision workflow consisting of coarse-grained interval localization, fine-grained detection, and iterative refinement. Within this workflow, different functional roles coordinate to support sequential decision-making. To support this progressive workflow, AnomaMind adopts a hybrid inference mechanism that combines general-purpose LLM-based reasoning with task-specific model learning. The general-purpose LLM is responsible for autonomous tool invocation and self-reflection, while anomaly detection decisions are optimized through reinforcement learning under workflow-level feedback. The following subsections detail each component.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Detection Tool Construction</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To support evidence-driven and sequential anomaly detection, AnomaMind is equipped with a structured collection of analytical tools that provide complementary capabilities for perception, numerical analysis, and contextual reasoning. Rather than embedding all functionality into a single model, these tools are designed as modular and reusable components that can be flexibly invoked during the detection workflow. Each tool focuses on a distinct aspect of anomaly analysis, enabling coarse evidence acquisition, fine-grained verification, and semantic interpretation to be carried out in a coordinated manner. Together, they form the foundation of the tool-augmented workflow, allowing anomaly detection decisions to be informed by diverse and progressively refined evidence.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Interval Localization</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The interval localization <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is designed to perform visual-aware interval localization on a normalized time series <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{1:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{1:T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically, this tool is implemented using a vision–language model (VLM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, which operates on visualized representations of the time series to perceptually identify anomalous regions. By analyzing visual cues such as shape irregularities, abrupt changes, and distributional shifts in the plotted sequences, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> produces a set of coarse-grained candidate intervals that potentially contain anomalous behavior. By leveraging visual perception rather than raw numerical comparison, the tool provides an initial narrowing of the search space, allowing subsequent stages to focus on informative regions instead of the entire sequence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Feature Extraction</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The feature extraction <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is responsible for fine-grained analysis of candidate intervals <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> through rigorous numerical examination.
Specifically, it performs statistical feature extraction as well as local and global structural pattern analysis, providing concrete numerical evidence to support subsequent anomaly confirmation or rejection.
These tools are sensitive to numerical deviations and structural irregularities, enabling precise discrimination between true anomalies and benign fluctuations.
By explicitly exposing verification operations, the framework ensures that anomaly detection decisions are grounded in measurable criteria, forming the basis for reliable fine-grained analysis and iterative refinement.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Knowledge Memory</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The knowledge memory <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> provides semantic and contextual support by supplying anomaly type explanations, domain knowledge descriptions, and tool semantics.
These tools offer high-level contextual features <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> that complement numerical evidence, helping the framework interpret intermediate patterns and guide subsequent decision-making.
By grounding detection results in domain-relevant knowledge, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> supports informed reasoning and facilitates tool-oriented planning.
This explicit grounding helps bridge the gap between numerical observations <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{1:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{1:T}</annotation></semantics></math> and interpretable anomaly characterization, improving reasoning consistency across interaction steps.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Coarse-to-Fine Detection Workflow</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">AnomaMind realizes anomaly detection through a structured coarse-to-fine workflow that unfolds over multiple interaction steps.
Rather than producing anomaly decisions in a single pass, the workflow progressively constructs diagnostic evidence and refines decisions as new information becomes available.
The workflow is organized into four key stages, each addressing a distinct role in the detection process: coarse evidence acquisition to localize suspicious intervals, adaptive evidence construction to gather informative cues, reasoning-based anomaly detection to produce fine-grained decisions, and iterative refinement to evaluate and revise intermediate outcomes.
These stages are tightly coupled and executed in a sequential manner, allowing earlier decisions to guide subsequent analysis while enabling principled revision when necessary.
Together, this coarse-to-fine design provides a unified mechanism for efficient localization, context-aware analysis, and accurate decision-making under complex and evolving conditions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Coarse Evidence Acquisition</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The workflow begins with coarse evidence acquisition, whose goal is to efficiently narrow down potentially anomalous regions from the full time series.
At this stage, AnomaMind leverages visual perception to identify suspicious temporal intervals based on salient pattern irregularities, abrupt changes, or distributional shifts.
Rather than performing precise anomaly judgments, this stage prioritizes recall and uncertainty awareness, producing a set of coarse candidate intervals that are likely to contain abnormal behavior.
By filtering out evidently normal regions early on, coarse evidence acquisition significantly reduces the subsequent search space and enables focused analysis.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Adaptive Evidence Construction</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the candidate intervals identified in the coarse stage, AnomaMind proceeds to adaptive evidence construction.
In this stage, large language models autonomously orchestrate tool interactions to gather informative numerical and contextual evidence relevant to the detected candidates.
The selection and invocation of tools are driven by intermediate observations and accumulated context, allowing different intervals to be analyzed using different diagnostic strategies.
This adaptive process enables flexible feature preparation and evidence accumulation, ensuring that subsequent detection decisions are grounded in task-relevant and context-aware information rather than fixed feature pipelines.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Anomaly Detection</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the constructed evidence, AnomaMind performs reasoning-based anomaly detection to produce fine-grained decisions.
This stage is responsible for assessing whether candidate intervals exhibit anomalous behavior, given the numerical features, structural patterns, and contextual cues collected earlier.
Unlike rule-based or purely discriminative approaches, the detection policy is optimized through reinforcement learning under workflow-level feedback, allowing it to adapt to the sequential and interdependent nature of detection decisions.
As a result, anomaly detection decisions reflect both local evidence and the broader diagnostic context accumulated across interaction steps.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Iterative Refinement</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">AnomaMind further incorporates an iterative refinement mechanism to evaluate and revise detection outcomes when necessary.
Through self-reflection, intermediate decisions are examined for potential inconsistency, uncertainty, or insufficient evidence.
If refinement is triggered, the workflow may return to earlier stages to acquire additional evidence, adjust analysis strategies, or reconsider candidate intervals.
This iterative process enables principled decision revision and prevents premature or brittle conclusions, allowing anomaly detection to progressively converge toward more reliable results.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>Hybrid Decision Mechanism</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">AnomaMind adopts a hybrid decision mechanism to support effective and stable inference across the coarse-to-fine detection workflow.
The key idea is to explicitly separate general-purpose reasoning from task-specific anomaly decision learning, allowing different components of the workflow to leverage their respective strengths.
Specifically, AnomaMind combines (i) general-purpose large language models for flexible reasoning and tool invocation, and (ii) a detection-specific anomaly decision module optimized through reinforcement learning under workflow-level feedback.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">General–Specialized Hybrid Reasoning</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In AnomaMind, general-purpose LLMs are responsible for driving reasoning-intensive and structurally flexible components of the workflow. These components include coarse-grained interval localization based on visual perception, autonomous invocation of feature extraction and diagnostic tools, and self-reflective assessment of intermediate detection results. Such stages require adaptive reasoning, dynamic control flow, and the ability to integrate heterogeneous evidence, which are well supported by general-purpose models without task-specific retraining. By contrast, these components do not require precise anomaly decision boundaries or strong task-specific inductive biases. Instead of enforcing rigid prediction objectives at this level, AnomaMind leverages the expressive reasoning capability of LLMs to flexibly explore suspicious regions, construct informative evidence, and guide subsequent decision steps. This design avoids over-constraining early-stage reasoning and enables the workflow to adapt to diverse anomaly patterns and contextual variations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reinforcement Learning for Anomaly Decision Learning</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While general-purpose reasoning is effective for evidence construction and workflow control, accurate anomaly detection decisions demand task-specific learning signals and stable optimization for precise boundary localization.
To this end, AnomaMind introduces a dedicated anomaly detection module whose decision policies are trained through reinforcement learning. Unlike prior approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite> that apply multi-turn reinforcement learning to train the entire detection workflow end-to-end, AnomaMind restricts reinforcement learning to this local decision module <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, significantly reducing training complexity. The reinforcement learning objective is defined based on workflow-level feedback rather than isolated prediction errors. Specifically, rewards are computed by evaluating the quality of anomaly detection decisions in the context of the full workflow, taking into account factors such as consistency with verification evidence, alignment with diagnostic outcomes, and the effectiveness of subsequent refinement steps. By grounding rewards in workflow-level feedback, the learned detection policy captures task-relevant inductive biases while remaining compatible with the surrounding reasoning-driven workflow.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce the experimental settings, followed by the main results, exploratory analyses, and finally a case study.</p>
</div>
