<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Experimental Settings</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.1. </span>Datasets</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ AnomaMind: Agentic Time Series Anomaly Detection with Tool-Augmented Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the statistics and annotation granularity of the datasets used in our experiments. We evaluate our method on four widely used TSAD benchmarks. Notably, P denotes point anomalies, while Seq indicates sequence anomalies. The Yahoo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laptev et al., 2015</a>)</cite> dataset released by Yahoo Labs consists of real and synthetic time series derived from production traffic. WSD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2022</a>)</cite> contains real-world key performance indicators collected from large-scale web services, while IOPS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2018</a>)</cite> includes system-level performance metrics reflecting service scale, quality, and machine health. TODS  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lai et al., 2021</a>)</cite> is a synthetic benchmark that covers diverse anomaly types, including point anomalies, contextual anomalies, and collective anomalies, and is designed to provide controlled yet challenging evaluation across varying temporal patterns and anomaly characteristics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.2. </span>Baselines</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare our method with a diverse set of state-of-the-art time-series anomaly detection approaches, spanning statistical-based, deep learning–based, foundation model–based, and LLM-based paradigms. The statistical baselines include FFT-AD<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                
                
                
              <a href="https://github.com/TimeEval/TimeEval-algorithms" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/TimeEval/TimeEval-algorithms</a></span></span></span>
and SR  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, which detects anomalies based on spectral characteristics in the frequency domain. Deep learning–based methods consist of CNN  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Munir et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, M2N2  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, LSTMAD  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Malhotra et al., 2015</a>)</cite>, and TransAD  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tuli et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, covering supervised, unsupervised, and test-time adaptive settings. We further include recent LLM-based approaches, including LLM-TSAD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., </a>)</cite>, LLMAD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and OFA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, which adapt large language models to time series anomaly detection through prompting, alignment, or in-context reasoning.
We also compare with foundation models, including TimesFM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Das et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and Chronos <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ansari et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which leverage large-scale pretraining to detect anomalies via forecasting deviations or direct anomaly inference.</p>
</div>
