<section id="S5.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.3. </span>Implemention Details</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our framework consists of several specialized components. For fine-grained reasoning, we employ Qwen3-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2023</a>)</cite> as the backbone, while all other modules use grok-4 as the default LLM. The workflow is optimized via full fine-tuning within the agent-lightning framework, with a learning rate of <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> and a batch size of 8, and trained on four NVIDIA A800 GPUs (80GB). We follow official implementations and hyperparameters for all baselines.
The goal of TSAD is to detect true anomalies while minimizing false alarms, requiring both high recall and precision. We therefore adopt F1 as the primary metric and additionally report Best-F1 by sweeping the detection threshold over a predefined range, following standard TSAD practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ AnomaMind: Agentic Time Series Anomaly Detection with Tool-Augmented Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, AnomaMind consistently demonstrates competitive average performance across the four evaluation metrics. The performance gains are particularly evident on datasets with more complex anomaly characteristics, where static detection methods often struggle to adapt to evolving temporal patterns and contextual dependencies. A closer examination across different baseline categories reveals several notable advantages of the proposed framework.
Conventional single-pass architectures exhibit limited effectiveness in these settings, suggesting that static input–output discrimination is insufficient for capturing evolving temporal contexts. Foundation models, while effective at achieving high recall, tend to suffer from reduced precision in zero-shot settings, reflecting a tendency to flag benign irregularities as anomalies in the absence of task-specific adaptation. In contrast, AnomaMind attains a more balanced precision–recall trade-off by adopting an agentic reinforcement learning strategy that enables the internalization of task-specific inductive biases.
Furthermore, compared with existing LLM-based detectors that lack explicit verification mechanisms and often display unstable numerical sensitivity, AnomaMind benefits from a tool-augmented reasoning workflow that supports iterative refinement and decision revision. Overall, these results suggest that reframing anomaly detection as a sequential, evidence-driven decision process—supported by adaptive reasoning, feedback-driven refinement, and specialized tool interaction—provides a promising alternative to conventional model-centric paradigms. conventional model-centric paradigms conventional model-centric paradigms</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>Ablation Studies</h3>

<section id="S5.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.3.1. </span>Detection Tool Ablation.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.1.2. Baselines ‣ 5.1. Experimental Settings ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ AnomaMind: Agentic Time Series Anomaly Detection with Tool-Augmented Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the full AnomaMind consistently outperforms all ablated variants across datasets, highlighting the effectiveness of the proposed detection tools. Removing any component leads to a clear performance drop, indicating that each tool contributes complementary information. In particular, excluding feature extraction causes the most severe degradation, underscoring its importance for informative representation learning, while removing knowledge memory or interval localization also degrades performance by limiting historical context modeling and coarse-grained candidate localization. Overall, these results suggest that coordinated tool interaction is essential for effective coarse-to-fine anomaly detection across diverse scenarios.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.13807v1/ablation-workflow.svg" id="S5.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/163;" width="476" height="163"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>Performance comparison with and without the evaluator in coarse-to-fine detection workflow.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.3.2. </span>Workflow Component Ablation.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5.3.1. Detection Tool Ablation. ‣ 5.3. Ablation Studies ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ AnomaMind: Agentic Time Series Anomaly Detection with Tool-Augmented Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, removing the evaluator results in a consistent performance degradation across all datasets, highlighting its importance in the proposed coarse-to-fine detection workflow. The degradation is more pronounced on IOPS and WSD, suggesting that the evaluator plays a key role in validating intermediate detection hypotheses and reducing false alarms through structured verification. In the absence of this component, detection decisions rely solely on coarse signals and are more susceptible to error accumulation across steps. By providing explicit feedback to refine earlier decisions, the evaluator enables the full framework to perform more reliable iterative refinement, leading to more stable detection performance across diverse time series scenarios.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.13807v1/rl_ablation.svg" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/159;" width="476" height="159"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>Performance comparison with and without reinforcement learning for anomaly decision learning.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.3.3. </span>Reinforcement Learning Ablation.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.3.2. Workflow Component Ablation. ‣ 5.3. Ablation Studies ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ AnomaMind: Agentic Time Series Anomaly Detection with Tool-Augmented Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, removing reinforcement learning results in a consistent and substantial performance degradation across all evaluated datasets, highlighting its importance in the proposed framework. Without reinforcement learning, the model relies primarily on fixed supervision signals and lacks the ability to revise or correct earlier decisions based on downstream outcomes.
By incorporating reinforcement learning, the agent is able to exploit outcome-driven feedback to refine its decision-making strategy over multiple steps. This allows the model to adjust its reasoning trajectory, suppress false alarms, and progressively improve anomaly judgments beyond coarse initial predictions. As a result, the full framework achieves consistently higher F1 and Best-F1 scores across datasets. Overall, these results indicate that reinforcement learning plays a crucial role in enabling adaptive policy optimization and iterative decision refinement within the agentic anomaly detection workflow.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4. </span>Exploration Analysis</h3>

<section id="S5.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.4.1. </span>Impact of Backbone Size.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Model scale plays a critical role in the performance of the proposed framework. As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.4.1. Impact of Backbone Size. ‣ 5.4. Exploration Analysis ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ AnomaMind: Agentic Time Series Anomaly Detection with Tool-Augmented Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, increasing the backbone size consistently improves detection performance across most datasets, with Qwen3-8B achieving the best F1 and Best-F1 scores. This trend suggests that larger models offer stronger representation and reasoning capacity, enabling more accurate anomaly characterization and refinement. In contrast, smaller variants show inferior performance on complex datasets, indicating limited capacity to capture intricate temporal patterns. Overall, these results demonstrate that sufficient model capacity is essential for fully leveraging the proposed agentic workflow.</p>
</div>
