</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.4.2. </span>Impact of Reward Component.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reward design plays a crucial role in guiding learning and final performance. As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 5.4.3. Impact of General-Purpose Models ‣ 5.4. Exploration Analysis ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ AnomaMind: Agentic Time Series Anomaly Detection with Tool-Augmented Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, the full model consistently outperforms variants that remove individual reward components. Removing the false-positive penalty causes a notable performance drop, indicating the importance of suppressing spurious alarms. Excluding the rule-matching component also degrades performance, highlighting its role in enforcing structural consistency. The most severe degradation occurs when the two-sided reward is removed, leading to near-random detection behavior and underscoring its fundamental role in stabilizing learning. Overall, these results confirm that the integration of all reward components is essential for accurate anomaly detection.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.4.3. </span>Impact of General-Purpose Models</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 5.4.3. Impact of General-Purpose Models ‣ 5.4. Exploration Analysis ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ AnomaMind: Agentic Time Series Anomaly Detection with Tool-Augmented Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, instantiating the proposed framework with different general-purpose model variants leads to observable performance, while the overall detection performance remains competitive. Grok-4 exhibits relatively strong performance across benchmarks, indicating its effectiveness as a general-purpose backbone. In contrast, Deepseek-V3.2 performs competitively on IOPS but shows a noticeable performance drop on WSD, reflecting a higher sensitivity to dataset characteristics and anomaly patterns. Overall, these results indicate that the proposed framework is not tightly coupled to a specific general-purpose model, while stronger backbones tend to yield more consistent detection performance across diverse scenarios.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5. </span> Impact of reward component design on reinforcement learning across different datasets.</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T5.4" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:62.2pt;vertical-align:-29.2pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-32.7pt,5.9pt) scale(0.840781083556086,0.840781083556086) ;">
