<td id="S5.T6.4.1.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T6.4.1.5.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline" style="font-size:90%;">0.410</span></td>
<td id="S5.T6.4.1.5.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T6.4.1.5.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">0.519</span></td>
<td id="S5.T6.4.1.5.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T6.4.1.5.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">0.523</span></td>
<td id="S5.T6.4.1.5.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T6.4.1.5.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">0.356</span></td>
<td id="S5.T6.4.1.5.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T6.4.1.5.9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">0.500</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.13807v1/case_ys.png" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:467/182;" width="467" height="182" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>An illustrative example of a structured coarse-to-fine workflow for agentic time series anomaly detection.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.4.4. </span>Impact of Different Specialized Modules.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.4.4. Impact of Different Specialized Modules. ‣ 5.4. Exploration Analysis ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ AnomaMind: Agentic Time Series Anomaly Detection with Tool-Augmented Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, comparing variants that only train the locator or the detector reveals that effective anomaly detection relies on training the detection component. While the LLM-based locator alone demonstrates a strong capability in identifying coarse anomaly candidates, its performance remains limited when reasoning-based anomaly detection is required. This suggests that the LLM lacks the task-specific inductive biases necessary for reliable anomaly discrimination.
In contrast, training the detector enables the model to internalize anomaly-specific decision boundaries, leading to consistently improved performance across datasets.
Overall, these results indicate that strong localization alone is insufficient and that explicitly training the detector is essential for compensating for the missing inductive biases of general-purpose LLMs. This further highlights the necessity of agentic anomaly detection frameworks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.13807v1/planning_ablation.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/158;" width="476" height="158"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5. </span>Evaluation of distinct specialized module variants during reinforcement learning.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.4.5. </span>Impact of Different Training Convergence.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.4.5. Impact of Different Training Convergence. ‣ 5.4. Exploration Analysis ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ AnomaMind: Agentic Time Series Anomaly Detection with Tool-Augmented Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, AnomaMind demonstrates stable training convergence across different datasets and backbone model scales. The reward consistently increases with training steps on all datasets, indicating effective policy optimization through iterative interaction and feedback. While convergence speed varies across datasets, the overall upward trends suggest stable learning behavior. In addition, larger backbone models tend to converge faster and achieve slightly higher rewards, while smaller models still exhibit smooth convergence.
Overall, these results confirm the effectiveness of the proposed framework. This stability further verifies the effectiveness of reinforcement learning–based optimization.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.13807v1/case-2.png" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/168;" width="476" height="168" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6. </span>Effect of different datasets and backbone models on training convergence.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5. </span>Case Studies</h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.4.3. Impact of General-Purpose Models ‣ 5.4. Exploration Analysis ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ AnomaMind: Agentic Time Series Anomaly Detection with Tool-Augmented Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> illustrates a representative example of the proposed tool-augmented, multi-turn agentic anomaly detection process on a periodic time series. The framework first performs coarse localization by combining visual cues with global sinusoidal trends, identifying a suspicious interval where a sharp acceleration disrupts the expected smooth pattern.
The locator then applies domain-aware verification by focusing on rate-of-change statistics while discarding unstable amplitude-based signals. Guided by this strategy, the actor extracts quantitative evidence using global and local difference Z-scores, revealing a dense outlier cluster indicative of sustained acceleration. The detector integrates multi-level evidence to confirm a violation of the smoothness constraint, and the evaluator further refines the anomaly boundaries by checking logical consistency across visual observations, tool outputs, and domain constraints. The final output is a refined anomaly interval with an interpretable diagnosis, demonstrating the effectiveness of multi-turn reasoning and tool interaction for precise anomaly detection.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we revisit TSAD from an agentic perspective and argue that effective anomaly detection should be treated as an evidence-driven diagnostic process rather than a static discriminative task. We propose AnomaMind, an agentic TSAD framework that reformulates anomaly detection as a sequential decision-making process with tool-augmented reasoning. By organizing detection into a structured coarse-to-fine workflow, AnomaMind enables adaptive feature preparation through multi-turn tool interactions and improves detection via self-reflective refinement. A key contribution is the hybrid inference mechanism, which decouples flexible reasoning from task-specific decision optimization, allowing general-purpose models to orchestrate tools while anomaly detection policies are learned through reinforcement learning under verifiable workflow-level feedback. Extensive experiments across diverse benchmarks demonstrate consistent performance gains, especially in complex scenarios. We believe AnomaMind represents a promising step toward more reliable, interpretable, and extensible TSAD systems and highlights the broader potential of agentic, tool-augmented reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
