<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Dataset Details</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To rigorously evaluate the effectiveness of our method, we selected four widely used benchmarks in the field of time series anomaly detection. These datasets span from real-world production data to diverse synthetic scenarios, covering a broad spectrum of anomaly types, including point, contextual, collective, and pattern anomalies. A detailed description of each dataset follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">YAHOO</span>: The Yahoo S5 dataset (2015) serves as a standard benchmark for univariate time series anomaly detection, derived from Yahoo’s production traffic. It comprises a real-world subset (A1) featuring genuine traffic spikes and drops, alongside three synthetic subsets (A2, A3, A4). These synthetic series are designed to mimic realistic temporal patterns while providing precise control over point, contextual, and collective anomalies.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TODS</span>: The TODS benchmark is a synthetic dataset designed to evaluate outlier detection under diverse and controlled scenarios. It contains univariate series featuring five distinct anomaly types from a behavior-driven taxonomy: point-global (single extreme deviations), pattern-contextual (locally anomalous subsequences), pattern-shapelet (unusual motifs), pattern-seasonal (disrupted periodicity), and pattern-trend (long-term trend deviations).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">IOPS</span>: The IOPS dataset (AIOps KPI Anomaly Detection dataset) is a widely adopted real-world benchmark for evaluating anomaly detection in cloud and web service monitoring. It features Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) such as traffic, latency, and CPU usage, reflecting the health and scale of massive online systems. The dataset contains hundreds of labeled anomalies—including spikes, drops, and drifts—encompassing point, contextual, and collective types, making it essential for assessing automated incident detection in complex IT environments.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">WSD</span>: The WSD dataset is a real-world benchmark consisting of KPIs collected from large-scale internet companies, including metrics like traffic, response times, and error rates. It reflects actual system health in high-traffic environments and is characterized by realistic production noise and a very low anomaly density (<math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>0.6%). The data includes labeled spikes, contextual shifts, and long-term drifts grounded in real incidents, often set against pronounced periodic and seasonal patterns.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Baseline Details</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To comprehensively evaluate our method, we benchmark against 10 competitive approaches, categorized into statistical methods, deep learning models, foundation models, and LLM-based agents.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Statistical Approaches.</span>
<span id="A2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FFT</span> detects anomalies by decomposing time series into frequency components. It retains low-frequency coefficients to reconstruct a smoothed signal, identifying anomalies through large deviations in high-frequency residuals.
<span id="A2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SR</span> (Spectral Residual) utilizes the Fourier transform to calculate log-amplitude deviations from local averages in the frequency domain. It then reconstructs the saliency map via inverse transform to pinpoint anomalous points.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Deep Learning Baselines.</span>
<span id="A2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CNN</span> employs 1D convolutional layers to extract local temporal features and forecasts future values via fully connected layers, detecting anomalies based on prediction error thresholds.
<span id="A2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LSTMAD</span> utilizes an LSTM-based encoder-decoder architecture to learn latent representations of normal patterns. It identifies anomalies by thresholding reconstruction errors, as deviations yield significantly higher losses.
<span id="A2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TransAD</span> leverages a Transformer encoder-decoder with multi-head self-attention to capture global temporal dependencies, using reconstruction error for anomaly detection.
<span id="A2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M2N2</span> is a test-time adaptation method that handles distribution shifts. It detrends sequences via exponential moving averages and selectively updates parameters on low-error instances through self-supervision.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Foundation Models.</span>
<span id="A2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TimesFM</span> is a decoder-only foundation model that patches input sequences into fixed-length tokens. It autoregressively generates future values, detecting anomalies via point-wise prediction errors.
<span id="A2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OFA</span> (One Fits All) utilizes a frozen pretrained transformer backbone with lightweight task-specific adapters. It processes sequences through the frozen encoder and uses adapter heads to reconstruct inputs for anomaly identification.
<span id="A2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Chronos</span> is a language-model-based forecaster that tokenizes time series values. Operating in a zero-shot setup, it produces probabilistic forecasts via a transformer encoder-decoder, flagging anomalies based on prediction deviations.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-based Approaches.</span>
<span id="A2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLMAD</span> employs in-context learning by retrieving similar time series segments as exemplars. It feeds these into an LLM with chain-of-thought prompts to directly infer anomaly locations, types, and explanations.
<span id="A2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-TSAD</span> decomposes time series into trend, seasonal, and residual components, converting them into text descriptions. Using few-shot prompts, the LLM reasons over the decomposed signals to identify anomalies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Extended Analysis and Experimental Results</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide a comprehensive analysis to further validate the effectiveness and accurateness of AnomaMind. We begin with a qualitative visualization comparison to demonstrate the model’s superior precision in localizing anomalies against baseline methods. Subsequently, we conduct a fine-grained ablation study on the verification tool components to elucidate the specific contributions of multi-granular evidence. Finally, we examine the model’s sensitivity to sampling temperature, verifying its stability and practicality for real-world deployment.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.13807v1/case_ys1.png" id="A3.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/360;" width="476" height="360" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7. </span>Visualization of detection results demonstrating the superior precision of AnomaMind compared to leading baselines.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1. </span>Visualization Case</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We visualize the detection results of our method alongside baseline approaches in Figure <a href="#A3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Appendix C Extended Analysis and Experimental Results ‣ AnomaMind: Agentic Time Series Anomaly Detection with Tool-Augmented Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. Observations indicate that while baselines such as FFT, TimesFM, M2N2, and LLM-TSAD successfully capture all anomalous regions, they suffer from a notably high false positive rate. Conversely, other baselines produce fewer false alarms but fail to detect the specific anomalous high plateau. In contrast, our approach, empowered by tool-augmented reasoning, accurately identifies all anomalous intervals with zero false positives, demonstrating superior precision and accuracy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2. </span>Feature Extraction Component Ablation</h3>

